# CTA Rules

The conversion surface is narrow. Every CTA on every page follows these rules so attribution works, buyers face one choice at a time, and no page drifts into "book a call or subscribe or download or chat" paralysis.

---

## The one-CTA principle

Each page has a **single primary CTA**: book a 15-minute intro call via Cal.com. That's it.

A contact form exists as a fallback for buyers who won't book on a cold link, but the form is structurally subordinate — below the fold, visually quieter, and the form's success state routes to the same next step (schedule the call).

No newsletter signup. No gated PDF. No "let's chat on WhatsApp." No three-option "which applies to you" menus. Every extra CTA halves conversion on the primary.

---

## CTA hierarchy across all four pages

| Level | Element | Where it appears | Visual weight |
|---|---|---|---|
| Primary | Cal.com 15-min intro booking button | Hero, mid-page, final CTA section (3 instances) | Full button, brand-primary background |
| Secondary | Contact form | One section near bottom of page | Form block, brand-muted background |
| Tertiary | LinkedIn link (Jon) | Footer only | Small text link |

Do not add a fourth CTA type without explicit reason tied to a specific buyer behavior we've observed.

