## CTA anti-patterns (catch these in Critique)

### Stacking CTAs on top of each other

> **Bad:** Hero block with "Book a call" button AND "Get the PDF" button AND "Email us" link.
> **Why:** Decision paralysis. The buyer chooses none.
> **Fix:** One primary CTA per block. Other paths appear lower on the page.

### Vague CTA verbs

> **Bad:** "Get in touch" / "Learn more" / "Contact us"
> **Why:** Doesn't tell the buyer what happens after the click.
> **Fix:** "Book a 15-minute intro call" — named, time-bounded, specific.

### Pressure language

> **Bad:** "Limited slots — book now before they're gone!" / "Offer ends Friday!"
> **Why:** Cheapens the brand. Energy buyers and MSP founders both see right through it.
> **Fix:** Honest risk-reversal: "15 minutes. If it's not a fit, we'll tell you."

### Form overload

> **Bad:** Form with 11 fields including dropdown for budget, company size, timeline, industry, and current tech stack.
> **Why:** Conversion drops 40%+ per extra field beyond 4-5.
> **Fix:** Name, Email, Company, Message. UTMs capture the rest.

### CTA without risk reversal

> **Bad:** Standalone "Book a call" button with no surrounding text.
> **Why:** Button asks for commitment. Buyer needs a reason to believe the commitment is low-cost.
> **Fix:** One-line risk-reversal sentence next to the button.

### Inconsistent Cal.com paths

> **Bad:** Some CTAs link to `cal.com/jonmc/intro`, others to `cal.com/jonmc/versafile-intro`.
> **Why:** Attribution breaks. We can't tell which motion the booking came from.
> **Fix:** Every CTA on a given page uses the same canonical path.

