## Post-launch iteration

After 4-6 weeks of live traffic, review CTA performance per page:

- **Click-through rate on Cal.com button** (target: >3% of page visitors)
- **Booking rate** (calendar shows up after click — target: >30% of clicks)
- **Form-to-booking rate** (of buyers who submit the form, how many also book — target: >40%)
- **UTM distribution** (which sources drive bookings vs. form-only)

If a page's CTR is below 1%, the problem is almost certainly copy upstream (hero, proof), not the button itself. Don't "redesign the button." Fix the copy that precedes it.

If a page's booking rate after click is low, the Cal.com event type page may be the problem — check that the event description, duration, and availability match what the buyer expects from the landing page.

---

## Summary — the contract for every page

Every page that goes live must satisfy:

1. One primary CTA (Cal.com booking) with three placements
2. One secondary fallback (contact form) with four fields
3. One tertiary (LinkedIn footer link)
4. Cal.com path is page-specific and consistent across the three placements
5. UTM capture + Cal.com enrichment + form POST metadata all wired
6. Motion tag matches the page
7. Risk-reversal sentence adjacent to every Cal.com button
8. Graceful form failure with direct email + Cal.com fallback

If any of these fail, the page isn't ready to ship.
